# SOEC Cell Performance Model

A physics-based python model of a Solid Oxide Electrolysis Cell (SOEC).
Built as a self-directed project alongside a Coursera course from the
Technical University of Denmark (DTU) on hydrogen key concepts and
green technologies.

---

## What it models

SOECs split water into green hydrogen and oxygen using electricity
at ~800°C. They are the most efficient electrolysis technology
available today.

|       Physics      |     Equation     |                 What it captures                 |
|        -----       |      ------      |                      ------                      |
|   Thermodynamics   |  Nernst Equation |      Min. Voltage to split water at temp. T      |
|      Ohmic Loss    |   Arrhenius ASR  | YSZ ceramic resistance - halves every ~50°C rise |
|   Activation Loss  |   Butler-Volmer  |        Energy barrier at electrode surface       |
| Concentration Loss |  Fick Diffusion  |      Gas starvation at high current density      |

Total Cell Voltage: **V_cell = V_ocv + η_ohm + η_act + η_con**

---

## Results

![SOEC Results](outputs/SOEC_Results.png)

| Temperature | OCV (V) | V @ 1 A/cm² | Efficiency |
|    -----    |   ---   |    -----    |    ----    |
|    750°C    |  1.021  |   2.107 V   |   70.2 %   |
|    800°C    |  1.010  |   2.016 V   |   73.4 %   |
|    850°C    |  1.000  |   1.931 V   |   76.7 %   |

---

## Validation

The model was validated against experimental i-U data from Jensen (2007) 
at 850°C with 50% H₂O + 50% H₂ inlet gas (Figure 21, Risø-PhD-29). 
Maximum error of ±1.1% across the full current density range (0-0.8 A/cm²), 
confirming the underlying physics is sound

---

## Current Version: Transient 1.5D

The current version extends the steady-state 1.5D architecture with full time-dependent 
governing equations, allowing the cell to respond dynamically to changes in operating 
conditions.

---

## Architecture

- 20 nodes along the flow path (x-axis)
- 5 layers through-plane per node: anode GDL → anode electrode → electrolyte →
  cathode electrode → cathode GDL
- Time derivatives on both energy and species equations
- Stiff ODE solver (Radau) to handle coupled thermal and electrochemical timescales

KEY FINDING: The electrochemical response (voltage, gas composition, fuel utilisation) 
settles in seconds. The thermal response takes several minutes. This timescale seperation 
is critical for renewable energy integration - input power can fluctuate rapidly, but the 
cell's thermal mass cannot keep up.

---

## Roadmap

|        Version        |                      What it adds                      |      Status      |
|         -----         |                         ------                         |      ------      |
|           0D          |           Single point, avg. gas composition           |       Done.      |
|           1D          |            Full variation along cell length            |       Done.      |
|          1.5D         |     Through-plane temp. & layer-by-layer gradients     |       Done.      |
|    Transient 1.5D     |   Time-resolved thermal and electrochemical response   |       Done.      |
| Transient + Renewable |              Real wind/solar input profile             |    In Progress   |
|           2D          |   Full spatial resolution: flow path + through-plane   |      Planned     |
|           3D          |     Full cell volume: ANSYS Fluent CFD validation      |      Planned     |

Following the modelling hierarchy used in published research from
DTU Energy (Nemati et al., 2024).

---

## How to run

```bash
pip install numpy matplotlib
python main.py
```

Output saves to `outputs/SOEC_Results.png`

---

## Project structure

SOEC_Project/
├── src/
|   ├── __init__.py
|   ├── cell_1d.py
|   ├── fuel_utilisation.py
|   ├── linkedin_plotter.py
|   ├── nernst.py             # Nernst OCV from thermodynamics
|   ├── overpotentials.py     # Butler-Volmer, Fick, Arrhenius losses
|   ├── plotter.py            # 4-panel results figure
|   ├── thermal_1d.py
|   ├── transient_solver.py
|   ├── validator.py
├── outputs/
|   └── SOEC_LinkedIn.png
|   ├── SOEC_Results.png
|   ├── SOEC_Transient_Results.png
├── run_transient.py
└── main.py

# Run this

Each file does one job. Physics, plotting, and parameters are fully
separated - changing a material property in one place updates 
everything automatically.

